# Orion Engineering and Security Rules

## 1. Engineering principles

- Treat Orion as an enterprise application handling potentially sensitive information.
- Follow secure-by-design and secure-by-default principles.
- Prefer modular architecture with clear separation of concerns.
- Avoid unnecessary microservices, frameworks and abstractions.
- Preserve existing application functionality unless a security issue requires a controlled change.
- Preserve the existing UI design, layout, branding and user experience.
- Use Python type annotations, meaningful docstrings and explicit interfaces.
- Use dependency injection where appropriate.
- Keep business logic out of API route handlers.
- Avoid circular dependencies, global mutable state and duplicated authorization logic.

## 2. Security boundaries

- Treat every external input as untrusted.
- Treat model output, retrieved content and tool output as untrusted.
- Never trust roles, permissions, tenant IDs or project claims supplied by the client.
- Enforce authorization on the backend and within sensitive application services.
- Apply object-level authorization to every sensitive resource.
- Use deny-by-default authorization.
- Do not rely on frontend visibility or disabled buttons for security.
- Never allow retrieved documents to modify system instructions or grant permissions.
- Never let a model decide whether it is authorized to perform an action.

## 3. Authentication and secrets

- Never introduce hardcoded credentials, tokens, API keys or signing secrets.
- Never accept unsigned authentication tokens.
- Validate token signature, issuer, audience and expiry.
- Obtain identity and authorization from trusted server-side sources.
- Do not enable development authentication in production.
- Fail closed when required production security configuration is missing.
- Keep provider credentials on the server.
- Do not expose secrets in logs, exceptions, responses or frontend code.

## 4. Agent and tool security

- All tool execution must pass through the centralized tool authorization boundary.
- Every tool must declare its schema, permissions, resource scope and risk level.
- Validate tool arguments and outputs.
- Restrict tools to explicit allowlists.
- Require approval for consequential actions.
- Treat retrieved content as data, never as executable instructions.
- Enforce tool call limits, execution timeouts and maximum agent steps.
- Detect repeated execution and agent loops.
- Use idempotency for side-effect operations.
- Audit authorization decisions and tool execution outcomes.
- Do not add arbitrary shell, filesystem or network tools.

## 5. FakeLLM and real model integration

- FakeLLM must remain deterministic and work without external credentials.
- FakeLLM must never execute instructions found in retrieved documents.
- Keep model providers behind a common interface.
- Model integration must be optional and explicitly configured.
- All inference must pass through the model gateway.
- Do not permit arbitrary model endpoints.
- Version prompts and record their content hashes.
- Separate system instructions, developer instructions, user input and retrieved context.
- Do not claim real-model behavior has been tested when FakeLLM was used.

## 6. RAG and data handling

- Authenticate and validate document ingestion.
- Preserve document provenance and classification.
- Enforce authorization before adding documents to model context.
- Prevent cross-user and cross-tenant retrieval.
- Track embedding and retrieval configuration versions.
- Treat retrieved content as potentially malicious.
- Validate file types, sizes and paths.
- Enforce canonical filesystem path containment.
- Do not expose files outside explicitly approved directories.
- Do not interpret local file fetching as SSRF unless external network requests are implemented.

## 7. AI evaluation and observability

- Maintain versioned evaluation datasets.
- Add regression tests for security-sensitive model and prompt behavior.
- Evaluate retrieval, groundedness, tool selection and authorization behavior.
- Include prompt injection, sensitive data disclosure and unsafe tool-use scenarios.
- Record model, prompt, embedding and evaluation suite versions.
- Use deterministic assertions wherever possible.
- Do not use LLM-as-judge as the sole evaluation mechanism.
- Correlate API, agent, model, retrieval and tool events.
- Do not log unrestricted prompts, credentials or sensitive documents by default.
- Record enough structured metadata to reconstruct security-relevant execution paths.

## 8. API and frontend

- Validate request and response schemas.
- Apply authentication, authorization, rate limits and bounded request sizes.
- Return safe error messages.
- Do not expose stack traces or internal implementation details.
- Use parameterized database queries.
- Treat model output as untrusted.
- Never render untrusted HTML directly.
- Apply contextual output encoding.
- Protect cookie-based sessions against CSRF.
- Configure CORS explicitly when required by the frontend.
- Do not expose backend secrets to browser code.

## 9. CI/CD and dependencies

- Maintain a reproducible dependency lockfile.
- Run tests and security checks in CI.
- Use meaningful security scanner exit codes.
- Do not suppress failed security checks using continue-on-error without an approved exception.
- Include SAST, SCA, secrets, container and IaC scanning where applicable.
- Generate and retain an SBOM.
- Distinguish confirmed vulnerabilities from scanner observations.
- Record accepted risks with owners and expiry dates.
- Never claim a clean scan when a scanner failed to execute.

## 10. Docker and infrastructure

- Run the application as a non-root numeric UID.
- Avoid privileged containers.
- Restrict writable filesystems and mounts.
- Do not embed secrets in images.
- Use minimal runtime images.
- Apply resource limits and health checks.
- Pin dependencies and base images appropriately.
- Keep production and development configurations separate.
- Preserve required persistent storage without granting unnecessary filesystem access.

## 11. Testing requirements

- Every security fix must have a regression test.
- Test both authorized and unauthorized paths.
- Include negative tests for authentication and authorization.
- Test path traversal and symlink escapes.
- Test agent tool abuse and prompt injection.
- Test tenant and object-level isolation.
- Test API rate limiting and input validation.
- Test production configuration fail-closed behavior.
- Use only local fixtures and safe stubs.
- Never test against external systems or real organizational data.
- Never send real emails or perform real consequential actions.
- Never weaken a test to make an implementation pass.

## 12. Change management

Before modifying security-sensitive code:

1. Understand the existing behavior.
2. Identify the trust boundary.
3. Identify the relevant threat.
4. Implement the smallest sound architectural change.
5. Add regression tests.
6. Run the relevant test suite.
7. Inspect the final diff.

Do not silently change security behavior.

Do not remove functionality without documenting the reason and safe replacement.

Do not claim a control is implemented unless the code and tests support that claim.

## 13. Production-readiness standard

Classify controls as:

- Implemented and tested.
- Implemented but not fully validated.
- Partially implemented.
- Dependent on external infrastructure.
- Deferred.
- Not applicable.

Production readiness requires evidence, not merely code changes or passing unit tests.

Always document residual risks and deployment prerequisites.